      en: "Paepae 'o Tele'a is the most architecturally impressive of the ancient stone tombs (langi) in the Lapaha District of Tongatapu. It was constructed in the 16th century for the 29th Tu'i Tonga, 'Uluakimata I, who was also known as Tele'a. The monument is distinguished by its precisely carved coral limestone blocks that form a multi-tiered pyramid. Particularly noteworthy are the L-shaped cornerstones, which were fitted together without mortar so exactly that a knife blade can hardly fit between them. The massive stone slabs had to be transported via large canoes from distant quarries, underscoring the empire's logistical capabilities. The tomb symbolizes the pinnacle of megalithic construction in Tonga and served as a model for subsequent royal burials. In Tongan culture, the site is considered highly sacred, and many rituals surrounding ancestor worship were held here. Today, Paepae 'o Tele'a is a central component of Tonga's World Heritage nomination. The structure's monumental presence brings the former grandeur of the Tu'i Tonga Empire to life for visitors.",
